    "question": {
      ":question/id": "de594c23-f0f5-5b5e-89ed-c8cf0bafa284",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=2 \\\\ &- z \\\\ &- \\frac{1}{y + 3} \\\\ &+\\frac{1}{\\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(z - 2\\right)} \\\\ &- \\frac{1}{\\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(y + 3\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$. The domain is $x\\ne 1,\\ y\\ne -3,\\ z\\ne 2$.\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=\\frac{- y + z - 5}{\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right) \\left(z - 2\\right)} \\\\ f_{y}&=\\frac{x}{\\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(y + 3\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_{z}&=-1 - \\frac{1}{\\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{- y + z - 5}{\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right) \\left(z - 2\\right)}&=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(y + 3\\right)^{2}}&=0 \\\\ -1 - \\frac{1}{\\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x - 1,\\ v=y + 3,\\ w=z - 2$. All denominator factors are nonzero. The second equation gives $u=-1$. Substituting into the third and then the first gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w^2&=1 \\\\ w&=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad w=-1 \\\\ v&=w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sign of $v$ must correspond to the same chosen value of $w$. Recover the original coordinates using $x=u + 1,\\ y=v - 3,\\ z=w + 2$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(0,-2,3)$ and $(0,-4,1)$. Each satisfies every component equation.",
