      ":question/id": "b70f45fb-b1a5-59e3-996c-5d7ef1d407a4",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=(x)\\ln(x)-2(x) \\\\ &+(y - 1)\\ln(y - 1)-2(y - 1) \\\\ &+(z - 2)\\ln(z - 2)-2(z - 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$. The domain is $x>0,\\ y>1,\\ z>2$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1 \\\\ f_{y}&=\\ln{\\left(y - 1 \\right)} - 1 \\\\ f_{z}&=\\ln{\\left(z - 2 \\right)} - 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1&=0 \\\\ \\ln{\\left(y - 1 \\right)} - 1&=0 \\\\ \\ln{\\left(z - 2 \\right)} - 1&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x,\\ v=y - 1,\\ w=z - 2$. The product rule gives $\\frac{d}{du}(u\\ln u)=\\ln u+1$ (and similarly for the other coordinates). Solve each logarithmic equation by exponentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln u&=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u=e \\\\ \\ln v&=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v=e \\\\ \\ln w&=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad w=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll resulting shifted coordinates are positive. Recover the original coordinates using $x=u,\\ y=v + 1,\\ z=w + 2$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(e,1 + e,2 + e)$. Each satisfies every component equation.",
