    "question": {
      ":question/id": "650ce993-1229-5d17-8ab2-7df2a77d17bf",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\left(z + 3\\right)^{2} e^{- z - 3} \\\\ &+x \\left(y + 2\\right) e^{2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$.\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=\\left(2 x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 2\\right) e^{2 x} \\\\ f_{y}&=x e^{2 x} \\\\ f_{z}&=\\left(- z - 1\\right) \\left(z + 3\\right) e^{- z - 3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\left(2 x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 2\\right) e^{2 x}&=0 \\\\ x e^{2 x}&=0 \\\\ \\left(- z - 1\\right) \\left(z + 3\\right) e^{- z - 3}&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x,\\ v=y + 2,\\ w=z + 3$. Exponential factors are positive. The second equation therefore gives $u=0$. Substituting in the first gives $v=0$. The third gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w \\left(2 - w\\right)&=0 \\\\ w&=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad w=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth branches must be kept. Recover the original coordinates using $x=u,\\ y=v - 2,\\ z=w - 3$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(0,-2,-3)$ and $(0,-2,-1)$. Each satisfies every component equation.",
